{"causes": ["The establishment of the independent Nizari Ismaili state at Alamut by Hasan-i Sabbah created the political entity Muhammad would inherit.", "Kiyā Buzurg-Ummīd's successful 14-year rule from 1138 solidified Nizari power and ensured a stable succession for his son.", "The political fragmentation and internal conflicts within the Seljuk Empire provided opportunities for the Nizari state to assert and maintain its autonomy."], "effects": ["Muḥammad ibn Buzurg-Ummīd's reign ensured the continued independence and territorial integrity of the Nizari Ismaili state for over two decades.", "He maintained the distinct religious and political identity of the Nizaris, allowing for the theological developments that would follow his rule.", "His stable leadership facilitated the succession of his son, Hasan II, whose declaration of Qiyamah (Resurrection) marked a pivotal shift in Nizari doctrine."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "kiya-buzurg-ummid", "sourceName": "Kiyā Buzurg-Ummīd", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "muhammad-ibn-buzurg-ummid", "targetName": "Muhammad ibn Buzurg-Ummid", "context": "Kiyā Buzurg-Ummīd's successful rule and established lineage enabled his son, Muḥammad, to succeed him as the third da'i of Alamut in 1138."}, {"sourceSlug": "muhammad-ibn-buzurg-ummid", "sourceName": "Muhammad ibn Buzurg-Ummid", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "alamut-castle", "targetName": "Alamut Castle", "context": "Muḥammad ibn Buzurg-Ummīd governed the Nizari Ismaili state from its central stronghold, Alamut Castle, throughout his 24-year reign."}, {"sourceSlug": "seljuk-empire", "sourceName": "Seljuk Empire", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "muhammad-ibn-buzurg-ummid", "targetName": "Muhammad ibn Buzurg-Ummid", "context": "The constant military and political pressure from various Seljuk factions significantly shaped Muḥammad ibn Buzurg-Ummīd's foreign policy and defensive strategies."}, {"sourceSlug": "muhammad-ibn-buzurg-ummid", "sourceName": "Muhammad ibn 